/*!  \page grib_defs grib_defs
\section DESCRIPTION
 Query the definitions in ecCodes concept files (GRIB edition 1 and 2) and print
 the matching entries, optionally with their GRIB encoding. A subcommand selects
 the concepts:
 \n
 - params: parameters, from the concept files paramId.def, shortName.def, name.def
   and units.def;
 - chems: chemical constituents, from the concept files chemId.def, chemShortName.def,
   chemName.def and chemFormula.def.
 \n
 The concept files are read below grib1/ and grib2/, including all localConcepts.
 For GRIB2 the variants used by the MTG2 switch are read as well: *.lte33.def
 (is_mtg2_switch=0), *.def (is_mtg2_switch=1) and *.chemsplit.def (is_mtg2_switch=2).
 \n
 params also reads the legacy concept files *.legacy.def in grib2/ and
 grib2/localConcepts/ecmf/, which ecCodes uses as the last fallback, after the files
 selected by the MTG2 switch. They apply to every MTG2 switch value, so each legacy entry
 is listed once, without an MTG2 switch (as for edition 1), and marked with is_legacy=1.
 --is_mtg2_switch therefore excludes them. Legacy entries are listed after all other
 GRIB2 entries. Legacy files in other directories are not
 used by ecCodes and are not read.
 \n
 Entries of the different concept files are joined into one record when edition,
 MTG2 switch, legacy flag, scope and encoding are identical. If several identifiers in
 the same scope have the same encoding, they are shown as a list, e.g.
 paramId=[500417, 500582]. A value which is not defined for an encoding is shown as '-'.
 \n
 Which definitions are read: without --definitions-root, the tool uses the same
 definitions path as ecCodes, i.e. ECCODES_EXTRA_DEFINITION_PATH, then
 ECCODES_DEFINITION_PATH or the built-in default, which may be MEMFS. Each concept
 file is looked up by ecCodes itself, so a file present in several directories is
 taken from the first one that has it, exactly as in ecCodes. The output therefore
 shows what the installed ecCodes actually uses.
 \n
 Parsing the concept files takes most of the run time. With --edition, --is_mtg2_switch,
 --is_legacy or --scope, only the files which can match are parsed, so these options
 make a query much faster. A concept file which cannot be parsed is then only reported
 if it is one of them, as in ecCodes, which only reads the files it needs.

\section USAGE
 grib_defs params|chems
 [options]
 \n
 Without a subcommand, the subcommands are listed. Without options, a short usage text
 of the subcommand is printed. To list all entries, give any option, e.g. --format line.

\section OPTIONS
 Options for both subcommands:
 \n \n -h, --help  \n
        Show the help text of the subcommand and exit.
 \n \n --definitions-root path \n
        Use only these definitions directories, instead of the ecCodes definitions path.
        Multiple paths can be supplied using the platform path separator
        (':' on Unix, ';' on Windows). A definition file present in several roots
        is taken from the first root that has it.
        Unlike ECCODES_DEFINITION_PATH, the built-in definitions are not appended and
        ECCODES_EXTRA_DEFINITION_PATH is ignored: only the given directories are read.
        This allows querying another definitions set on its own, e.g. the definitions
        directory of an older ecCodes version, to find out since when a parameter exists.
 \n \n --debug-path-resolution \n
        Print the ecCodes definitions path, its directories and the
        backend used (filesystem, memfs or mixed) to stderr. Also reports concept files
        read from disk which a MEMFS build would not find, and how many concept files
        were parsed and skipped by the filters.
 \n \n --columns col1,col2,... \n
        Output only selected columns.
        Valid columns for params: edition,is_mtg2_switch,is_legacy,scope,paramId,shortName,name,units,encoding,sources
        \n
        Valid columns for chems: edition,scope,chemId,chemShortName,chemName,chemFormula,encoding,sources
 \n \n --scope scope1,scope2,... \n
        Comma-separated list of exact scopes to include,
        e.g. grib2,grib2/localConcepts/ecmf. An unknown scope is reported together
        with the list of available scopes.
 \n \n --regex-case-sensitive \n
        Make regex filters case-sensitive. By default they are case-insensitive.
        The -regex filters use the ECMAScript syntax of C++ std::regex,
        matched anywhere in the value; use ^ and $ to anchor.
 \n \n --attr key=value,key=value,... \n
        Filter by encoding. A record matches when its encoding contains at least
        the listed key/value pairs. Array values are written as --show-encoding
        prints them, e.g. typeOfStatisticalProcessing=[102,2].
 \n \n --attr-strict \n
        With --attr and/or --attrkey, require an exact match: the encoding must have
        exactly the listed keys, with the values given in --attr.
 \n \n --attrkey key1,key2,... \n
        Keep only records whose encoding contains all listed keys, with any value.
 \n \n --nattrkey key1,key2,... \n
        Exclude records whose encoding contains any of the listed keys.
 \n \n --nattr key=value,key=value,... \n
        Exclude records whose encoding contains any of the listed key/value pairs.
 \n \n --show-encoding \n
        Print encoding key/value constraints for each result.
 \n \n --show-sources \n
        Print source definition files contributing to each result.
 \n \n --format line|table|json \n
        Output format. Default is line. json prints an array with one object per
        result. Values of merged duplicates are arrays, missing values are null,
        --show-encoding adds an encoding object and --show-sources a sources array.
 \n \n --order asc|desc \n
        Sort order. Default is asc. Without --order-by, params records are sorted by
        edition, MTG2 switch, scope, paramId and is_legacy, chems records by
        edition, scope and chemId.
 \n \n --order-by field1,field2,... \n
        Sort fields in priority order. Valid fields are the columns without
        encoding and sources.
 \n \n --no-truncate \n
        With --format table, show cells in full. By default long cells are cut to a
        fixed maximum width per column (e.g. name 48, encoding 60 characters).
 \n \n
 Options for params only:
 \n \n --paramId pattern, --paramId-regex regex \n
        Wildcard or regex filter for paramId.
 \n \n --shortName pattern, --shortName-regex regex \n
        Wildcard filter (case-insensitive), e.g. '10*', or regex filter for shortName.
 \n \n --name pattern, --name-regex regex \n
        Wildcard filter (case-insensitive), e.g. '*precipitation*', or regex filter for name.
 \n \n --units pattern, --units-regex regex \n
        Wildcard filter (case-insensitive) or regex filter for units.
 \n \n --is_mtg2_switch 0|1|2 \n
        Restrict GRIB2 records to lte33=0, def=1, chemsplit=2. Legacy entries have no
        MTG2 switch and are excluded.
 \n \n --is_legacy 0|1 \n
        Restrict GRIB2 records to entries from *.legacy.def files (1) or from all
        other concept files (0).
 \n \n --edition 1|2 \n
        Restrict to GRIB edition 1 or 2.
 \n \n
 Options for chems only:
 \n \n --chemId pattern, --chemId-regex regex \n
        Wildcard or regex filter for chemId.
 \n \n --chemShortName pattern, --chemShortName-regex regex \n
        Wildcard filter (case-insensitive), e.g. 'CH*', or regex filter for chemShortName.
 \n \n --chemName pattern, --chemName-regex regex \n
        Wildcard filter (case-insensitive), e.g. '*oxide*', or regex filter for chemName.
 \n \n --chemFormula pattern, --chemFormula-regex regex \n
        Wildcard filter (case-insensitive) or regex filter for chemFormula.
 \n \n
 An option of the other subcommand is rejected with a hint to that subcommand.
 \n \n
\section grib_defs_examples grib_defs examples
-# List all entries for paramId 130:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --paramId 130
\endverbatim

-# Show encoding constraints for exact WMO temperature triplet in GRIB2:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --edition 2 --attr discipline=0,parameterCategory=0,parameterNumber=0 --attr-strict --show-encoding
\endverbatim

-# Show only selected columns in table format:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --name '*temperature*' --columns edition,scope,paramId,shortName,encoding --format table
\endverbatim

-# Parameters of one centre, as used with the MTG2 switch set to 1:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --scope grib2/localConcepts/ecmf --is_mtg2_switch 1
\endverbatim

-# GRIB2 parameters with a statistical process, sorted by shortName:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --edition 2 --attrkey typeOfStatisticalProcessing --order-by shortName
\endverbatim

-# Legacy entries of ECMWF local concepts, as a table:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --is_legacy 1 --scope grib2/localConcepts/ecmf --format table
\endverbatim

-# List all entries, then only encodings shared by several paramIds:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --format line
grib_defs params --format line | grep 'paramId=\['
\endverbatim

-# Encoding of paramId 167 as JSON, e.g. for processing with jq:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --paramId 167 --edition 2 --show-encoding --format json
\endverbatim

-# Since which ecCodes version does paramId 263006 exist? Query the definitions of
   each version on their own (here from clones of the tagged versions):\n
\verbatim
for v in 2.38.0 2.39.0 2.40.0; do
    printf '%s: ' $v
    grib_defs params --definitions-root /path/to/eccodes-$v/definitions --paramId 263006 > /dev/null && echo yes || echo no
done
\endverbatim

-# Check which definitions are read:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs params --paramId 167 --debug-path-resolution
\endverbatim

-# Chemical constituents with a given formula:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs chems --chemFormula O3
\endverbatim

-# Filter by chemId regex:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs chems --chemId-regex '^1[0-9]+$'
\endverbatim

-# Show the encoding of all oxides in table format:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs chems --chemName '*oxide*' --columns chemId,chemShortName,chemName,encoding --format table --no-truncate
\endverbatim

-# Entries for a formula as JSON:\n
\verbatim
grib_defs chems --chemFormula O3 --format json
\endverbatim
*/
